			## 'Proof of Human' Is Hiring: At Least the Name Is Honest About the Fantasy

			Ah, the return of the classic: a YC S23 startup called 'Proof of Human' is hiring a Member of Technical Staff. Because nothing says 'we have a real product' like a company name that sounds like a philosophical thought experiment. I've seen this before — expert systems, big data, blockchain, now 'proof of humanity.' The demos are always spectacular. The production deployments are always a slide deck. Show me the prod deployment, and maybe I'll stop laughing.

			The name alone is a red flag. 'Proof of Human' — what, are we running out? In the 80s it was 'knowledge engineers.' In the 2000s, 'data scientists.' In the 2010s, 'trustless consensus.' Now it's 'proof of human,' which is a great title for a sci-fi novel and a terrible one for an actual technical solution. I'm sure it's a nice wrapper around some CAPTCHA and a browser fingerprint library. I'd bet the company's core technical innovation is just adding 'blurry spinning nonsense' to a login page.

			And they're hiring a 'Member of Technical Staff' — the title that screams 'we want to sound like Bell Labs but we're literally three dudes in a WeWork.' That means they don't have a product yet, or they have one and the original engineer left after realizing the founders' 'vision' was just 'we'll figure it out.' If history is any guide, this person will build the entire system, get a 'mission-driven' equity package, and then watch the company pivot to 'AI-powered enterprise identity solutions' in six months, followed by a quiet retraction of every claim they made.

			I'm not anti-AI; I'm anti-BS. The irony is that we're inventing 'proof of human' to stop bots that are themselves AI, while the actual humans are busy writing breathless tweets about how this will 'change everything.' I'm sure that will work great in production — just like the expert systems that were supposed to replace doctors, the Hadoop clusters that were supposed to derive meaning from 'unstructured data,' and the blockchain supply chains that were supposed to revolutionize tracking. All of them ended up in the same place: a forgotten repo and a founder who moved on to 'advisory roles.' Good luck to the MTS hire. You'll need it.
			"""
